    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "BLUF",
      "narration_text": "Here is the big picture. Language models do not see letters or words. They only see tokens — which are just pre-packaged text chunks. When an entire word is glued together into a single token, the AI can no longer see the letters trapped inside it. That is why letter-counting breaks down.",
      "voice": "am_puck",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "estimated_duration_s": 20,
      "shot": {
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "BrutalistHesitantWriter",
          "props": {
            "text": "The unit is a word.\nWait — no.\nThe unit is a token.\nNot a letter. Not a word.\nJust a frequent chunk\nof characters.",
            "face": "serif",
            "align": "center"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": "2026-09-28T02:04:21.085606+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "actual_duration_s": 16.45,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 16.458333333333332,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:BrutalistHesitantWriter",
        "at": "2026-09-27T23:51:12"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "myth",
      "narration_text": "Here's the myth. You type 'strawberry,' and somewhere inside the model, it sees s, t, r, a, w, b, e, r, r, y — ten separate letters, ready to count. It doesn't.",
      "voice": "am_puck",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "actual_duration_s": 11.29,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 11.291666666666666,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-09-27T23:51:12"
      },
      "shot": {
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "SLATE",
          "props": {}
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "why tokens",
      "narration_text": "You might wonder: why not just build the AI to read letter by letter? It comes down to computation speed. If the model had to process text one letter at a time, reading a paragraph would take forever, and it would quickly forget the beginning of a sentence. Tokens are a mathematical compromise: they compress text so the AI can process huge amounts of data quickly, but at the cost of character-level precision.",
      "voice": "am_puck",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "estimated_duration_s": 15,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-09-27T23:51:12"
      },
      "actual_duration_s": 18.97,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 19.0,
      "shot": {
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "SLATE",
          "props": {}
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "define",
      "narration_text": "So what does it read instead? It uses Byte Pair Encoding. Imagine an algorithm scanning millions of books. It finds letters that sit together—like t and h—and glues them into th. Then it sees th and e sit together, and glues them into the. It repeats this until common words become single, solid blocks. Now, the AI can process the entire word the in one lightning-fast step, instead of three slow letters. That is the speed advantage.",
      "voice": "am_puck",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "actual_duration_s": 20.35,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 20.375,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ExecutedData",
        "at": "2026-09-27T23:51:12"
      },
      "shot": {
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ExecutedData",
          "props": {
            "title": "BPE: Merging Letters",
            "mode": "table",
            "columnLabels": [
              "Process",
              "Chunk"
            ],
            "rows": [
              {
                "label": "t + h",
                "value": "th",
                "at": 0
              },
              {
                "label": "th + e",
                "value": "the",
                "at": 1
              }
            ],
            "note": "Common words become solid blocks."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B04.mp4",
            "at": "2026-09-28T02:05:09.802225+00:00"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "not linguistic",
      "narration_text": "Here is the difference it makes. If an AI read the sentence \"the strawberry is red\" letter by letter, it would take twenty-one separate processing steps. By using tokens, it takes exactly four. That is the double-edged sword: the AI reads lightning fast, but because \"strawberry\" is fused into a single solid block, the model becomes completely blind to the letters trapped inside it.",
      "voice": "am_puck",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "actual_duration_s": 18.9,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 18.916666666666668,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ExecutedData",
        "at": "2026-09-27T23:51:12"
      },
      "shot": {
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ExecutedData",
          "props": {
            "title": "\"the strawberry is red\"",
            "mode": "table",
            "columnLabels": [
              "Unit",
              "Steps needed"
            ],
            "rows": [
              {
                "label": "Letters (t-h-e-...)",
                "value": 21,
                "at": 0
